---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/profile';
---

<Base
  title="About this site"
  description="Architecture, pipeline and costs of this site: S3, CloudFront, Terraform and GitHub Actions."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About this site</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      [FILL: 1-2 sentences saying the site is also an infrastructure project, provisioned and shipped with the same
      practices used at work.]
    </p>

    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <pre class="diagram" aria-label="Architecture diagram">
Visitor ──HTTPS──▶ Route 53 ──▶ CloudFront (CDN + TLS/ACM)
                                    │  OAC (private access)
                                    ▼
                              S3 (private bucket)

GitHub ──push──▶ GitHub Actions ──OIDC──▶ AWS
                  ├─ lint + scan → plan (PR) / apply (main)
                  └─ Astro build → s3 sync → invalidation
                     (deploy only after apply)</pre>
    <p>
      [FILL: one paragraph walking through a request. Facts to cover: static site built with Astro; Route 53 alias
      records (apex + www) pointing to CloudFront; ACM certificate validated via DNS; private S3 bucket reachable only
      through Origin Access Control; a CloudFront Function that redirects www → apex and rewrites /page/ to
      /page/index.html; security headers (HSTS, CSP, X-Frame-Options, Referrer-Policy) via a response headers
      policy; HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, TLS 1.2+.]
    </p>

    <h2>Infrastructure as code</h2>
    <p>
      [FILL: explain the two Terraform stacks. "bootstrap" is the only manual step and runs once: remote-state S3
      bucket (versioned, encrypted, native S3 locking), GitHub OIDC provider, least-privilege IAM roles and the
      GitHub repository variables themselves (via the github provider). The role trust policies are pinned to the
      repository's immutable OIDC subject (owner and repo numeric IDs). "main" creates the S3 bucket, CloudFront,
      ACM, DNS records, an AWS Budget alert and SSM parameters consumed by the pipeline.]
    </p>

    <h2>Pipeline</h2>
    <p>
      [FILL: describe the single pipeline (pipeline.yml). It detects whether a change touches infra, site or both;
      runs terraform fmt, validate, tflint and checkov; on pull requests it posts the terraform plan as a comment;
      on main it applies and only then deploys (Astro build, sync with long immutable cache for hashed assets and
      no-cache for HTML, CloudFront invalidation). The deploy job reads the bucket and distribution ID from SSM
      Parameter Store, so even the first deploy is fully automated. Highlight OIDC with no access keys stored in
      GitHub and separate least-privilege roles (the deploy role can only write to the bucket and invalidate the
      cache).]
    </p>

    <h2>Costs</h2>
    <p>
      [FILL: real monthly/yearly cost. Estimate: .com domain ~US$15/year, Route 53 hosted zone US$0.50/month,
      S3 + CloudFront + ACM ≈ US$0 at portfolio traffic. Mention the AWS Budget alert at US$5/month. Ask the owner
      for the actual figure from Cost Explorer before publishing; if unknown, present it as an estimate.]
    </p>

    <h2>Decisions and next steps</h2>
    <p>
      [FILL: optional. Why a static site instead of a server, why Astro, and possible next steps (e.g. Lighthouse
      checks in CI, WAF, access logs).]
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href={`${profile.links.github}/portfolio`} rel="noopener" target="_blank">View the source on GitHub →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
